// Generated by tools/build-docs.mjs from docs/*.md. Do not edit: change the Markdown and run npm run docs.
export var DOC_HTML = "<p>Pages: <a href=\"#models\" data-doc>Overview and models</a> | <a href=\"#config-reference\" data-doc>Config reference</a> | <a href=\"#accuracy\" data-doc>Accuracy and limits</a> | <a href=\"#glossary\" data-doc>Glossary</a></p>\n<hr>\n<section>\n<h2 id=\"models\">Project Wednesday</h2>\n<p>Project Wednesday is a simple orbital simulator that can model satellite orbits. Enjoy.</p>\n<p>This page describes what the simulator models. The other pages are the <a href=\"#config-reference\" data-doc>config reference</a> (every config field and every <code>plan.csv</code> line), <a href=\"#accuracy\" data-doc>accuracy and limits</a> (how good the results are and how to choose a step size) and the <a href=\"#glossary\" data-doc>glossary</a>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"models--frames-and-time\">Frames and time</h3>\n<ul><li><b>Earth (<code>earth_default</code>).</b> The inertial frame is the GCRF: Earth-centred, with ICRF axes. The body-fixed frame is the ITRF, reached with IAU 2006 precession, IAU 2000B nutation (the 77 luni-solar terms of <code>NUT00B</code>) and the Earth rotation angle, with UT1 = UTC + <code>earth_orientation.dut1_s</code>. Polar motion is not applied.</li><li><b>Uniform sphere (<code>sphere_uniform</code>).</b> The inertial frame has ICRF axes centred on the body. The body spins at a fixed rate about a pole given by its right ascension and declination, with the prime meridian angle given at the start epoch (IAU convention).</li><li><b>Time.</b> The integrator runs in TT seconds from J2000. Epochs can be given in UTC, TAI, TT, TDB or GPS. UTC uses the IERS leap-second table kept as data in <code>index.html</code>: TT = UTC + ΔAT + 32.184 s.</li></ul>\n<h3 id=\"models--integrators\">Integrators</h3>\n<ul><li><b>RK4</b> (<code>\"type\": \"rk4\"</code>): the classic fixed-step 4th-order Runge-Kutta method. All satellites step together. Output rows between steps come from cubic Hermite interpolation.</li><li><b>DOP853</b> (<code>\"type\": \"dop853\"</code>): the Dormand-Prince 8(5,3) method with adaptive step size. Each step is sized so its estimated error stays within <code>rtol</code> × |value| + <code>atol</code>, and output rows come from its 7th-order dense output. Each satellite gets its own steps. Steps land exactly on burn times, and, when they apply, on every shadow entry and exit and every 3-hour space-weather change, so a sudden change in force never falls inside a step.</li></ul>\n<p>Either way the output step is independent of the integration step. See <a href=\"#accuracy--choosing-a-step\" data-doc>accuracy and limits</a> for how to choose.</p>\n<h3 id=\"models--forces\">Forces</h3>\n<p>The acceleration is the sum of the terms that are switched on:</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\"><span class=\"math\"><b>a</b> = <b>a</b><sub>grav</sub> + <b>a</b><sub>drag</sub> + <b>a</b><sub>srp</sub> + <b>a</b><sub>3b</sub></span></p>\n<h4 id=\"models--gravity\">Gravity</h4>\n<p>For Earth, the WGS84 ellipsoid with the zonal harmonics J2, J3 and J4 (EGM96 values), about the true pole of date. For a uniform sphere, point-mass gravity μ/r². Tesseral harmonics (the east-west lumps in Earth's field) are not modelled.</p>\n<h4 id=\"models--atmospheric-drag\">Atmospheric drag</h4>\n<p>Active when <code>force_model.drag</code> is true and the satellite is below 1,500 km:</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\"><span class=\"math\"><b>a</b><sub>drag</sub> = -½ C<sub>d</sub> (A/m) ρ v<sub>rel</sub> <b>v</b><sub>rel</sub></span></p>\n<p>where <b>v</b><sub>rel</sub> is the velocity relative to air that rotates with the Earth. A satellite that falls to 80 km is declared re-entered. The density ρ comes from one of two models (<code>force_model.atmosphere</code>):</p>\n<ul><li><b>Exponential</b> (the default): Vallado's piecewise-exponential table (US Standard Atmosphere 1976 up to 25 km, CIRA-72 above). A fixed, moderate-activity profile with no solar activity, so real decay rates can differ by a factor of several.</li><li><b>NRLMSISE-00</b> (<code>\"nrlmsise00\"</code>): the NRL empirical model (Picone et al. 2002), which varies with latitude, local time, season, solar flux and geomagnetic activity. Drag at 400 km is about ten times stronger at solar maximum than at minimum, and doubles again in a major storm. Inputs are the previous day's observed F10.7, its 81-day centred mean and the 3-hour ap history, from a CelesTrak space-weather table bundled with the page. The table holds measured values to 20 July 2025, then CelesTrak's daily and monthly predictions. Monthly predictions have no Ap, so Ap = 15 is used there, and beyond October 2041 F10.7 = 150 and Ap = 15. The setup warnings say when predicted or default values are used, and <code>force_model.space_weather</code> replaces any of them with your own. Nothing is fetched from the network.</li></ul>\n<h4 id=\"models--solar-radiation-pressure\">Solar radiation pressure</h4>\n<p>Active when <code>force_model.srp</code> is true. The cannonball model, pushing directly away from the Sun:</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\"><span class=\"math\"><b>a</b><sub>srp</sub> = -P<sub>srp</sub> (1 AU / r<sub>☉</sub>)<sup>2</sup> C<sub>r</sub> (A/m) <b>û</b><sub>☉</sub></span></p>\n<p>with P<sub>srp</sub> = 4.56 × 10<sup>-6</sup> N/m² at 1 AU, scaled by the Earth-Sun distance r<sub>☉</sub> (about ±3.4% over a year). It is switched off inside a cylindrical Earth shadow, with no penumbra. C<sub>r</sub> can follow a schedule. <code>\"solar_drag\"</code> is accepted as another name for <code>\"srp\"</code>, though SRP is radiation pressure, not drag.</p>\n<h4 id=\"models--sun-and-moon-gravity\">Sun and Moon gravity</h4>\n<p>Active when <code>force_model.third_body</code> is true. The Sun and Moon act as point masses. The simulation's origin is the Earth's centre, which they also accelerate, so the force on the satellite is their direct pull minus their pull on the Earth:</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\"><span class=\"math\"><b>a</b><sub>3b</sub> = Σ GM<sub>b</sub> [ (<b>r</b><sub>b</sub> - <b>r</b>) / |<b>r</b><sub>b</sub> - <b>r</b>|<sup>3</sup> - <b>r</b><sub>b</sub> / |<b>r</b><sub>b</sub>|<sup>3</sup> ]</span></p>\n<p>GM values are from JPL DE440. The Moon's position comes from the Meeus series as implemented in ERFA <code>eraMoon98</code> (about 3\" RMS, 6 km). The Sun's comes from the same low-precision formula used for SRP and shadows (about 30\", 10,000 km), which is ample for a perturbation this size. In LEO the effect is tens to hundreds of metres a day. In GEO it is the main perturbation, tilting the orbit by roughly 0.75 to 0.95° a year.</p>\n<h3 id=\"models--inputs\">Inputs</h3>\n<p>Initial orbits can be osculating Keplerian elements, an inertial position and velocity, or a two-line element set (TLE). A TLE is propagated to the start epoch with SGP4 (SDP4 for periods of 225 minutes or more), converted from TEME to GCRF, and from then on the simulator's own force model takes over, so the orbit drifts away from what SGP4 would predict. Constellations can be generated as a Walker pattern or a train. Burns are impulsive, in LVLH axes (radial, along-track, cross-track) or inertial axes.</p>\n<h3 id=\"models--run-report-and-checks\">Run report and checks</h3>\n<div class=\"doc-tablewrap\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><tr><th>Metric</th><th>Meaning</th></tr><tr><td>Energy drift</td><td>The specific orbital energy is checked at every step. The work done by drag, SRP and Sun and Moon gravity, and the energy added by burns, are tracked and removed, so what is left is numerical error alone (typically below 10<sup>-8</sup>). For a uniform sphere the Jacobi integral is checked too.</td></tr><tr><td>Period, perigee, apogee</td><td>From the osculating orbit at the start epoch. Altitudes are above the equatorial radius.</td></tr><tr><td>LTAN</td><td>Local time of the ascending node: the mean local solar time when the satellite crosses the equator going north, with the node measured on the true equator of date. The drift rate is a least-squares line through the samples, and needs at least 3 orbits and 12 hours.</td></tr><tr><td>Beta angle</td><td>The angle between the Sun direction and the orbit plane.</td></tr><tr><td>Burns, Δv</td><td>Number of burns and total delta-v per satellite.</td></tr></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"models--source-licence-and-attribution\">Source, licence and attribution</h3>\n<p>Project Wednesday is made by Kshitij Khandelwal. More at <a href=\"https://kshitijkhandelwal.com\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">kshitijkhandelwal.com</a>.</p>\n<p>The source code is on GitHub at <a href=\"https://github.com/khandelwalkshitij/project-wednesday\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">github.com/khandelwalkshitij/project-wednesday</a>. Report bugs or request features through the <a href=\"https://github.com/khandelwalkshitij/project-wednesday/issues\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">issue tracker</a>. The <a href=\"https://github.com/khandelwalkshitij/project-wednesday/blob/main/CHANGELOG.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">changelog</a> lists what changed in each version.</p>\n<p><b>Project Wednesday</b> is open-source software released under the <a href=\"https://opensource.org/licenses/MIT\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">MIT License</a>. The software is provided \"as is\", without warranty of any kind.</p>\n<ul><li><b>Standards:</b> IAU 2006/2000B precession-nutation, the IERS Bulletin C leap-second table, WGS84 and EGM96 zonal coefficients.</li><li><b>SGP4/SDP4:</b> from <a href=\"https://github.com/shashwatak/satellite-js\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">satellite.js</a> 6.0.2 (MIT), a port of Vallado's reference code.</li><li><b>Atmosphere and space weather:</b> NRLMSISE-00 (Picone, Hedin, Drob and Aikin 2002), ported from the public-domain C version by Dominik Brodowski. A U.S. Government work not subject to copyright; no endorsement by the Naval Research Laboratory is implied. Space weather from <a href=\"https://celestrak.org/SpaceData/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">CelesTrak</a>.</li><li><b>Moon ephemeris, nutation and TDB series:</b> coefficients from <a href=\"https://github.com/liberfa/erfa\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ERFA</a> (BSD-style licence, derived from IAU SOFA).</li><li><b>Map:</b> continental outlines from <a href=\"https://www.naturalearthdata.com/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Natural Earth</a> (public domain).</li><li><b>Graphics:</b> <a href=\"https://threejs.org/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">three.js</a> (MIT).</li></ul>\n</section>\n<hr>\n<section>\n<h2 id=\"config-reference\">Config reference</h2>\n<p>A run is described by one JSON config. The JSON box in the app (\"Edit config as JSON\") is the source of truth, and the setup form edits it. The same config is written into every <code>plan.csv</code>, so a run can be repeated exactly. Every example on this page is a complete config: paste it into the JSON box and press Run. The test suite runs each one.</p>\n<p>Before a run, the whole config is checked and every problem is listed by field name. Warnings (a coarse step, predicted space weather and so on) are listed too, but do not stop the run.</p>\n<h3 id=\"config-reference--field-summary\">Field summary</h3>\n<div class=\"doc-tablewrap\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><tr><th>Field</th><th>Meaning</th></tr><tr><td><code>parent</code></td><td>Required. <code>{\"shape\": \"earth_default\"}</code>, or <code>{\"shape\": \"sphere_uniform\", \"radius_m\", \"density_kg_m3\", \"rotation\": {...}}</code> for a uniform body. <a href=\"#config-reference--parent\" data-doc>Details</a></td></tr><tr><td><code>earth_orientation.dut1_s</code></td><td>Optional, Earth only. UT1 minus UTC in seconds, -0.9 to 0.9. Default 0.</td></tr><tr><td><code>start_epoch</code></td><td>Required. <code>\"YYYY-MM-DDThh:mm:ss.sss SCALE\"</code>, SCALE one of UTC, TAI, TT, TDB, GPS.</td></tr><tr><td><code>duration</code> or <code>end_epoch</code></td><td>One of them. Seconds, or an ISO 8601 duration such as <code>\"P1DT6H\"</code>; or an end epoch in the start_epoch format.</td></tr><tr><td><code>integrator</code></td><td>Required. <code>{\"type\": \"rk4\", \"time_step_s\": 10}</code> (fixed step) or <code>{\"type\": \"dop853\", \"rtol\": 1e-10, \"atol\": 1e-6}</code> (adaptive; optional <code>max_step_s</code>, <code>initial_step_s</code>). <a href=\"#config-reference--integrator\" data-doc>Details</a></td></tr><tr><td><code>output_step_s</code></td><td>Required. Spacing of rows in plan.csv, independent of the integration step.</td></tr><tr><td><code>force_model</code></td><td>Optional; gravity is always on. <code>\"gravity_only\"</code>, or an object with <code>drag</code>, <code>srp</code>, <code>third_body</code>, <code>atmosphere</code>, <code>space_weather</code>. <a href=\"#config-reference--force_model\" data-doc>Details</a></td></tr><tr><td><code>satellites</code></td><td>Required. <code>count</code>, <code>mass_kg</code>, <code>cd</code>, <code>cr</code>, <code>area_m2</code>, <code>names</code> or <code>name_prefix</code>, and <code>orbits</code> or <code>pattern</code>. <a href=\"#config-reference--satellites\" data-doc>Details</a></td></tr><tr><td><code>satellites.orbits[ ]</code></td><td>One per satellite: <code>\"keplerian\"</code>, <code>\"cartesian\"</code> or <code>\"tle\"</code>, each able to override <code>mass_kg</code>, <code>cd</code>, <code>cr</code>, <code>area_m2</code>. <a href=\"#config-reference--orbits\" data-doc>Details</a></td></tr><tr><td><code>satellites.pattern</code></td><td>Instead of <code>orbits</code>: a Walker constellation or a train. <a href=\"#config-reference--pattern\" data-doc>Details</a></td></tr><tr><td><code>maneuvers[ ]</code></td><td>Optional impulsive burns: <code>{\"sat\", \"t_s\" or \"epoch\", \"dv_m_s\": [a, b, c], \"frame\": \"lvlh\" or \"inertial\"}</code>. <a href=\"#config-reference--maneuvers\" data-doc>Details</a></td></tr></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"config-reference--a-minimal-config\">A minimal config</h3>\n<p>One satellite in a 500 km orbit around Earth for a day, with the fixed-step integrator:</p>\n<pre><code>{\n  \"parent\": { \"shape\": \"earth_default\" },\n  \"start_epoch\": \"2026-11-10T06:30:00.000 UTC\",\n  \"duration\": \"P1D\",\n  \"integrator\": { \"type\": \"rk4\", \"time_step_s\": 10 },\n  \"output_step_s\": 60,\n  \"satellites\": {\n    \"count\": 1,\n    \"mass_kg\": 100,\n    \"orbits\": [\n      { \"type\": \"keplerian\", \"a_m\": 6878137, \"e\": 0.001, \"i_deg\": 53, \"raan_deg\": 0, \"argp_deg\": 0, \"ta_deg\": 0 }\n    ]\n  }\n}</code></pre>\n<h3 id=\"config-reference--parent\">parent</h3>\n<div class=\"doc-tablewrap\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><tr><th>Field</th><th>Type and limits</th><th>Notes</th></tr><tr><td><code>shape</code></td><td><code>\"earth_default\"</code> or <code>\"sphere_uniform\"</code></td><td><code>\"custom\"</code> (a mesh body) is not available in this build.</td></tr><tr><td><code>radius_m</code></td><td>number > 0</td><td><code>sphere_uniform</code> only.</td></tr><tr><td><code>density_kg_m3</code></td><td>number > 0</td><td><code>sphere_uniform</code> only. μ = G × mass follows from radius and density.</td></tr><tr><td><code>rotation.spin_period_s</code></td><td>number > 0</td><td><code>sphere_uniform</code> only.</td></tr><tr><td><code>rotation.pole_ra_deg</code></td><td>number</td><td>Right ascension of the spin pole, in ICRF.</td></tr><tr><td><code>rotation.pole_dec_deg</code></td><td>-90 to 90</td><td>Declination of the spin pole.</td></tr><tr><td><code>rotation.prime_meridian_deg</code></td><td>number</td><td>Rotation angle W of the prime meridian at the start epoch.</td></tr></table></div>\n<p><code>earth_default</code> takes no other fields. It is the WGS84 ellipsoid with J2 to J4, Earth rotation, an atmosphere and a dipole magnetic field (shown in the telemetry). <code>earth_orientation: {\"dut1_s\": ...}</code> sets UT1 minus UTC for Earth.</p>\n<p>A small asteroid-like body, with a satellite 1 km from its centre:</p>\n<pre><code>{\n  \"parent\": {\n    \"shape\": \"sphere_uniform\", \"radius_m\": 250, \"density_kg_m3\": 1900,\n    \"rotation\": { \"spin_period_s\": 15480, \"pole_ra_deg\": 0, \"pole_dec_deg\": 90, \"prime_meridian_deg\": 0 }\n  },\n  \"start_epoch\": \"2026-11-10T06:30:00.000 UTC\",\n  \"duration\": \"P1D\",\n  \"integrator\": { \"type\": \"rk4\", \"time_step_s\": 10 },\n  \"output_step_s\": 300,\n  \"satellites\": {\n    \"count\": 1,\n    \"mass_kg\": 10,\n    \"names\": [\"PROBE\"],\n    \"orbits\": [\n      { \"type\": \"keplerian\", \"a_m\": 1000, \"e\": 0, \"i_deg\": 30, \"raan_deg\": 0, \"argp_deg\": 0, \"ta_deg\": 0 }\n    ]\n  }\n}</code></pre>\n<h3 id=\"config-reference--time\">Time</h3>\n<div class=\"doc-tablewrap\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><tr><th>Field</th><th>Type and limits</th><th>Notes</th></tr><tr><td><code>start_epoch</code></td><td><code>\"YYYY-MM-DDThh:mm:ss.sss SCALE\"</code></td><td>SCALE is UTC, TAI, TT, TDB or GPS. Years 1900 to 2200; UTC from 1972 on. A seconds value of 60 is allowed only in UTC during a leap second.</td></tr><tr><td><code>duration</code></td><td>number of seconds > 0, or ISO 8601</td><td><code>\"P1D\"</code>, <code>\"PT6H\"</code>, <code>\"P1DT6H30M\"</code>, <code>\"P2W\"</code>; decimals are allowed.</td></tr><tr><td><code>end_epoch</code></td><td>epoch, same format as <code>start_epoch</code></td><td>Must be after the start. Give <code>duration</code> or <code>end_epoch</code>, not both. The run length is measured in TT, so an end epoch in TDB can fall a few microseconds short of a whole number of output steps.</td></tr><tr><td><code>output_step_s</code></td><td>number > 0</td><td>Rows are written at the start and every <code>output_step_s</code> after it. A run may write at most 1,500,000 rows in total.</td></tr></table></div>\n<p>A run given by start and end epochs in TT, with a UT1 correction:</p>\n<pre><code>{\n  \"parent\": { \"shape\": \"earth_default\" },\n  \"earth_orientation\": { \"dut1_s\": 0.05 },\n  \"start_epoch\": \"2026-11-10T06:30:00.000 TT\",\n  \"end_epoch\": \"2026-11-10T18:30:00.000 TT\",\n  \"integrator\": { \"type\": \"rk4\", \"time_step_s\": 10 },\n  \"output_step_s\": 120,\n  \"satellites\": {\n    \"count\": 1,\n    \"mass_kg\": 100,\n    \"orbits\": [\n      { \"type\": \"keplerian\", \"a_m\": 7078137, \"e\": 0.001, \"i_deg\": 98.2, \"raan_deg\": 120, \"argp_deg\": 0, \"ta_deg\": 0 }\n    ]\n  }\n}</code></pre>\n<h3 id=\"config-reference--integrator\">integrator</h3>\n<div class=\"doc-tablewrap\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><tr><th>Field</th><th>Type and limits</th><th>Notes</th></tr><tr><td><code>type</code></td><td><code>\"rk4\"</code> or <code>\"dop853\"</code></td><td></td></tr><tr><td><code>time_step_s</code></td><td>number > 0</td><td><code>rk4</code> only. A step above 25% of an orbit's period is rejected; above 5% gives a warning.</td></tr><tr><td><code>rtol</code></td><td>1e-13 to below 1</td><td><code>dop853</code> only. Allowed error per step as a fraction of each value. 1e-10 is a good default; 1e-12 for reference runs.</td></tr><tr><td><code>atol</code></td><td>number > 0</td><td><code>dop853</code> only. Allowed absolute error per step, in metres for position and m/s for velocity. 1e-6 suits most runs.</td></tr><tr><td><code>max_step_s</code></td><td>number > 0</td><td><code>dop853</code>, optional. Caps the step. Default: no cap.</td></tr><tr><td><code>initial_step_s</code></td><td>number > 0</td><td><code>dop853</code>, optional. Default: chosen automatically.</td></tr></table></div>\n<p>RK4 runs at most 300 million satellite-steps; DOP853 stops with an error if it would need more, or if its step becomes too small to make progress. <a href=\"#accuracy--choosing-a-step\" data-doc>Accuracy and limits</a> has a table of the error each setting gives.</p>\n<p>A Molniya orbit with the adaptive integrator, which takes small steps at perigee and large ones at apogee:</p>\n<pre><code>{\n  \"parent\": { \"shape\": \"earth_default\" },\n  \"start_epoch\": \"2026-11-10T06:30:00.000 UTC\",\n  \"duration\": \"P2D\",\n  \"integrator\": { \"type\": \"dop853\", \"rtol\": 1e-10, \"atol\": 1e-6, \"max_step_s\": 600 },\n  \"output_step_s\": 300,\n  \"satellites\": {\n    \"count\": 1,\n    \"mass_kg\": 1500,\n    \"names\": [\"MOLNIYA-1\"],\n    \"orbits\": [\n      { \"type\": \"keplerian\", \"a_m\": 26600000, \"e\": 0.72, \"i_deg\": 63.4, \"raan_deg\": 40, \"argp_deg\": 270, \"ta_deg\": 0 }\n    ]\n  }\n}</code></pre>\n<h3 id=\"config-reference--force_model\">force_model</h3>\n<p>Gravity is always on. Leave <code>force_model</code> out, or set it to <code>\"gravity_only\"</code>, for gravity alone. Every other force needs <code>parent.shape</code> <code>\"earth_default\"</code>.</p>\n<div class=\"doc-tablewrap\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><tr><th>Field</th><th>Type</th><th>Notes</th></tr><tr><td><code>drag</code></td><td>true or false</td><td>Atmospheric drag below 1,500 km; re-entry is declared at 80 km. Needs an area (<code>satellites.area_m2</code> or per orbit). Cd defaults to 2.2.</td></tr><tr><td><code>atmosphere</code></td><td><code>\"exponential\"</code> or <code>\"nrlmsise00\"</code></td><td>Needs <code>drag: true</code>. Default <code>\"exponential\"</code>.</td></tr><tr><td><code>space_weather</code></td><td><code>{\"f107\", \"f107a\", \"ap\"}</code></td><td>Needs <code>\"atmosphere\": \"nrlmsise00\"</code>. Any subset: F10.7 and its 81-day mean (above 0, at most 1000), daily Ap (0 to 400). What is left out comes from the bundled table. Fixing <code>ap</code> uses the daily-Ap form of the model.</td></tr><tr><td><code>srp</code></td><td>true or false</td><td>Solar radiation pressure with a cylindrical shadow. Needs an area. Cr defaults to 1.8. <code>solar_drag</code> is accepted as another name.</td></tr><tr><td><code>third_body</code></td><td>true or false</td><td>Sun and Moon gravity.</td></tr></table></div>\n<p>Drag with NRLMSISE-00 during the May 2024 storm, using the bundled space-weather table:</p>\n<pre><code>{\n  \"parent\": { \"shape\": \"earth_default\" },\n  \"start_epoch\": \"2024-05-10T12:00:00.000 UTC\",\n  \"duration\": \"P1D\",\n  \"integrator\": { \"type\": \"dop853\", \"rtol\": 1e-10, \"atol\": 1e-6 },\n  \"output_step_s\": 300,\n  \"force_model\": { \"drag\": true, \"atmosphere\": \"nrlmsise00\" },\n  \"satellites\": {\n    \"count\": 1,\n    \"mass_kg\": 100,\n    \"cd\": 2.2,\n    \"area_m2\": 1,\n    \"orbits\": [\n      { \"type\": \"keplerian\", \"a_m\": 6778137, \"e\": 0.0005, \"i_deg\": 51.6, \"raan_deg\": 10, \"argp_deg\": 0, \"ta_deg\": 0 }\n    ]\n  }\n}</code></pre>\n<p>The same model with fixed solar and geomagnetic activity, and SRP with a Cr that ramps from 1.8 to 2.0 over the first day (a sail deploying, say). Cr between schedule entries is interpolated linearly and held flat outside them:</p>\n<pre><code>{\n  \"parent\": { \"shape\": \"earth_default\" },\n  \"start_epoch\": \"2026-11-10T06:30:00.000 UTC\",\n  \"duration\": \"P2D\",\n  \"integrator\": { \"type\": \"rk4\", \"time_step_s\": 10 },\n  \"output_step_s\": 300,\n  \"force_model\": { \"drag\": true, \"srp\": true, \"atmosphere\": \"nrlmsise00\", \"space_weather\": { \"f107\": 120, \"f107a\": 120, \"ap\": 12 } },\n  \"satellites\": {\n    \"count\": 1,\n    \"mass_kg\": 50,\n    \"cd\": 2.2,\n    \"area_m2\": 2,\n    \"cr\": [[0, 1.8], { \"epoch\": \"2026-11-11T06:30:00.000 UTC\", \"cr\": 2.0 }],\n    \"orbits\": [\n      { \"type\": \"keplerian\", \"a_m\": 6928137, \"e\": 0.001, \"i_deg\": 97.6, \"raan_deg\": 80, \"argp_deg\": 0, \"ta_deg\": 0 }\n    ]\n  }\n}</code></pre>\n<p>A geostationary satellite with Sun and Moon gravity, where they are the main perturbation:</p>\n<pre><code>{\n  \"parent\": { \"shape\": \"earth_default\" },\n  \"start_epoch\": \"2026-11-10T06:30:00.000 UTC\",\n  \"duration\": \"P30D\",\n  \"integrator\": { \"type\": \"dop853\", \"rtol\": 1e-10, \"atol\": 1e-6 },\n  \"output_step_s\": 3600,\n  \"force_model\": { \"third_body\": true },\n  \"satellites\": {\n    \"count\": 1,\n    \"mass_kg\": 3000,\n    \"names\": [\"GEO-1\"],\n    \"orbits\": [\n      { \"type\": \"keplerian\", \"a_m\": 42164000, \"e\": 0.0002, \"i_deg\": 0.05, \"raan_deg\": 20, \"argp_deg\": 0, \"ta_deg\": 0 }\n    ]\n  }\n}</code></pre>\n<h3 id=\"config-reference--satellites\">satellites</h3>\n<div class=\"doc-tablewrap\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><tr><th>Field</th><th>Type and limits</th><th>Notes</th></tr><tr><td><code>count</code></td><td>integer 1 to 100</td><td>May be left out when it follows from <code>orbits</code> or a Walker pattern.</td></tr><tr><td><code>mass_kg</code></td><td>above 0, at most 10,000</td><td>Shared mass. Required here or on every orbit.</td></tr><tr><td><code>cd</code></td><td>number > 0</td><td>Drag coefficient. Default 2.2.</td></tr><tr><td><code>cr</code></td><td>number above 0, at most 3, or a schedule</td><td>Reflectivity coefficient (1 absorbs all light, 2 is a mirror). Default 1.8. A schedule is a list of <code>[t_s, cr]</code>, <code>{\"t_s\", \"cr\"}</code> or <code>{\"epoch\", \"cr\"}</code> entries.</td></tr><tr><td><code>area_m2</code></td><td>number > 0</td><td>Cross-section for drag and SRP. Required when either is on.</td></tr><tr><td><code>names</code></td><td>list of <code>count</code> names</td><td>Unique, non-empty, without commas, quotes, #, ; or =.</td></tr><tr><td><code>name_prefix</code></td><td>text</td><td>Instead of <code>names</code>: satellites are called PREFIX-01, PREFIX-02 and so on. Default <code>\"SAT\"</code>.</td></tr><tr><td><code>orbits</code></td><td>list of <code>count</code> orbits</td><td>See below.</td></tr><tr><td><code>pattern</code></td><td>Walker or train</td><td>Instead of <code>orbits</code>. See below.</td></tr></table></div>\n<h4 id=\"config-reference--orbits\">orbits</h4>\n<div class=\"doc-tablewrap\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><tr><th>Type</th><th>Fields</th><th>Notes</th></tr><tr><td><code>\"keplerian\"</code></td><td><code>a_m</code> (> 0), <code>e</code> (0 to below 1), <code>i_deg</code> (0 to 180), <code>raan_deg</code>, <code>argp_deg</code>, <code>ta_deg</code></td><td>Osculating elements in the parent's inertial frame at the start epoch.</td></tr><tr><td><code>\"cartesian\"</code></td><td><code>r_m: [x, y, z]</code>, <code>v_m_s: [vx, vy, vz]</code></td><td>Inertial position and velocity at the start epoch.</td></tr><tr><td><code>\"tle\"</code></td><td><code>line1</code>, <code>line2</code></td><td>Earth only. Checksums are checked. SGP4 gives the state at the start epoch; the simulator's own force model takes it from there.</td></tr></table></div>\n<p>Every orbit may also give its own <code>mass_kg</code>, <code>cd</code>, <code>cr</code> and <code>area_m2</code>, overriding the shared values. A satellite may not start below the surface, or below 80 km when drag is on.</p>\n<p>Three satellites from Keplerian elements, a state vector and a TLE, two with their own properties:</p>\n<pre><code>{\n  \"parent\": { \"shape\": \"earth_default\" },\n  \"start_epoch\": \"2026-10-28T00:00:00.000 UTC\",\n  \"duration\": \"PT12H\",\n  \"integrator\": { \"type\": \"rk4\", \"time_step_s\": 10 },\n  \"output_step_s\": 120,\n  \"force_model\": { \"drag\": true },\n  \"satellites\": {\n    \"count\": 3,\n    \"mass_kg\": 150,\n    \"area_m2\": 1.5,\n    \"names\": [\"KEP-1\", \"STATE-1\", \"ISS-LIKE\"],\n    \"orbits\": [\n      { \"type\": \"keplerian\", \"a_m\": 6928137, \"e\": 0, \"i_deg\": 53, \"raan_deg\": 0, \"argp_deg\": 0, \"ta_deg\": 0, \"cd\": 2.4 },\n      { \"type\": \"cartesian\", \"r_m\": [6878137, 0, 0], \"v_m_s\": [0, 4612.5, 6038.2], \"mass_kg\": 80, \"area_m2\": 0.5 },\n      { \"type\": \"tle\",\n        \"line1\": \"1 25544U 98067A   26300.50000000  .00016717  00000-0  10270-3 0  9991\",\n        \"line2\": \"2 25544  51.6416 247.4627 0006703 130.5360 325.0288 15.50125391 56316\" }\n    ]\n  }\n}</code></pre>\n<h4 id=\"config-reference--pattern\">pattern</h4>\n<p><b>Walker delta</b> (<code>\"type\": \"walker\"</code>): <code>t</code> satellites (1 to 100) in <code>p</code> evenly spaced planes (<code>p</code> must divide <code>t</code>), phasing <code>f</code> from 0 to p - 1 (default 0), all at <code>a_m</code> and <code>i_deg</code>. Optional <code>e</code> (default 0), <code>raan0_deg</code>, <code>ta0_deg</code> and <code>argp_deg</code> (default 0). Plane k has RAAN <code>raan0_deg</code> + 360k/p; satellite j in it has true anomaly <code>ta0_deg</code> + 360j/(t/p) + 360fk/t. <code>count</code>, if given, must equal <code>t</code>.</p>\n<pre><code>{\n  \"parent\": { \"shape\": \"earth_default\" },\n  \"start_epoch\": \"2026-11-10T06:30:00.000 UTC\",\n  \"duration\": \"PT6H\",\n  \"integrator\": { \"type\": \"rk4\", \"time_step_s\": 10 },\n  \"output_step_s\": 120,\n  \"satellites\": {\n    \"mass_kg\": 260,\n    \"name_prefix\": \"WALKER\",\n    \"pattern\": { \"type\": \"walker\", \"a_m\": 6928137, \"i_deg\": 53, \"t\": 24, \"p\": 3, \"f\": 1 }\n  }\n}</code></pre>\n<p><b>Train</b> (<code>\"type\": \"train\"</code>): <code>count</code> satellites in one base orbit (Keplerian fields), each offset from the one before by exactly one of <code>raan_deg</code>, <code>ta_deg</code> or <code>along_track_m</code> (an arc length along the orbit, converted to true anomaly at the base orbit's semi-major axis).</p>\n<pre><code>{\n  \"parent\": { \"shape\": \"earth_default\" },\n  \"start_epoch\": \"2026-11-10T06:30:00.000 UTC\",\n  \"duration\": \"PT6H\",\n  \"integrator\": { \"type\": \"rk4\", \"time_step_s\": 10 },\n  \"output_step_s\": 60,\n  \"satellites\": {\n    \"count\": 4,\n    \"mass_kg\": 100,\n    \"name_prefix\": \"TRAIN\",\n    \"pattern\": {\n      \"type\": \"train\",\n      \"base\": { \"a_m\": 6878137, \"e\": 0.001, \"i_deg\": 97.4, \"raan_deg\": 30, \"argp_deg\": 0, \"ta_deg\": 0 },\n      \"offset\": { \"along_track_m\": 50000 }\n    }\n  }\n}</code></pre>\n<h3 id=\"config-reference--maneuvers\">maneuvers</h3>\n<p>A list of impulsive burns, each applied at its exact time.</p>\n<div class=\"doc-tablewrap\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><tr><th>Field</th><th>Type and limits</th><th>Notes</th></tr><tr><td><code>sat</code></td><td>a satellite name</td><td></td></tr><tr><td><code>t_s</code> or <code>epoch</code></td><td>seconds after the start (0 or more), or an epoch</td><td>One of them. The burn must fall inside the run.</td></tr><tr><td><code>dv_m_s</code></td><td>three numbers, m/s</td><td>For <code>\"lvlh\"</code>: radial, along-track, cross-track. For <code>\"inertial\"</code>: x, y, z in the inertial frame.</td></tr><tr><td><code>frame</code></td><td><code>\"lvlh\"</code> or <code>\"inertial\"</code></td><td>Default <code>\"lvlh\"</code>.</td></tr></table></div>\n<p>Two satellites in a train; one raises its orbit with two along-track burns, then makes a small inertial correction:</p>\n<pre><code>{\n  \"parent\": { \"shape\": \"earth_default\" },\n  \"start_epoch\": \"2026-11-10T06:30:00.000 UTC\",\n  \"duration\": \"P1D\",\n  \"integrator\": { \"type\": \"rk4\", \"time_step_s\": 10 },\n  \"output_step_s\": 60,\n  \"satellites\": {\n    \"count\": 2,\n    \"mass_kg\": 100,\n    \"names\": [\"CHASER\", \"TARGET\"],\n    \"pattern\": {\n      \"type\": \"train\",\n      \"base\": { \"a_m\": 6778137, \"e\": 0, \"i_deg\": 51.6, \"raan_deg\": 0, \"argp_deg\": 0, \"ta_deg\": 0 },\n      \"offset\": { \"ta_deg\": 2 }\n    }\n  },\n  \"maneuvers\": [\n    { \"sat\": \"CHASER\", \"t_s\": 3600, \"dv_m_s\": [0, 1.5, 0] },\n    { \"sat\": \"CHASER\", \"epoch\": \"2026-11-10T08:15:00.000 UTC\", \"dv_m_s\": [0, 1.5, 0], \"frame\": \"lvlh\" },\n    { \"sat\": \"CHASER\", \"t_s\": 43200, \"dv_m_s\": [0.1, -0.2, 0.05], \"frame\": \"inertial\" }\n  ]\n}</code></pre>\n<h3 id=\"config-reference--warnings\">Warnings</h3>\n<p>These do not stop a run:</p>\n<ul><li><b>Coarse step:</b> an RK4 step above 5% of an orbit's period.</li><li><b>High eccentricity:</b> e above 0.85 with RK4, where a small step is needed at periapsis.</li><li><b>Near-equatorial orbit:</b> inclination within 0.05° of the equator, where the ascending node and LTAN are undefined.</li><li><b>Space weather:</b> with NRLMSISE-00, when any part of the run uses CelesTrak's predictions or the defaults rather than measured values.</li></ul>\n<h3 id=\"config-reference--plancsv-reference\">plan.csv reference</h3>\n<p>Every run writes <code>plan.csv</code>: comment lines starting with <code>#</code>, a column header, then one row per satellite per output time. The app can open a saved <code>plan.csv</code> again (\"Open plan.csv\"), and reruns its config from the <code># config:</code> line.</p>\n<h4 id=\"config-reference--header-lines\">Header lines</h4>\n<div class=\"doc-tablewrap\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><tr><th>Line</th><th>Content</th></tr><tr><td><code># sim_version:</code></td><td>The simulator version that wrote the file.</td></tr><tr><td><code># inertial_frame:</code></td><td><code>GCRF (ICRF axes)</code> for Earth; <code>ICRF axes</code> for a sphere. The centre is the parent's centre of mass.</td></tr><tr><td><code># body_fixed_frame:</code></td><td>How the body-fixed frame is defined (for Earth: ITRF via IAU 2006/2000B and the Earth rotation angle, no polar motion).</td></tr><tr><td><code># integrator:</code></td><td><code>rk4; time_step_s=...</code>, or <code>dop853; rtol=...; atol=...; accepted_steps; rejected_steps; shadow_events; step_s_min; step_s_max</code>.</td></tr><tr><td><code># force_model:</code></td><td>Every force in use, with its model (atmosphere, space-weather source, shadow, ephemerides).</td></tr><tr><td><code># parent_pole_ra_deg:</code>, <code># parent_pole_dec_deg:</code></td><td>The parent's pole at the start epoch.</td></tr><tr><td><code># data_file:</code></td><td>One line per data set used: <code>name; version; crc32; source</code>. Always the leap-second table and the built-in models; also the NRLMSISE-00 coefficients, the space-weather table and the Moon series when those are used.</td></tr><tr><td><code># config:</code></td><td>The full config, as one line of JSON.</td></tr><tr><td><code># column:</code></td><td>Name, unit and frame of each column.</td></tr><tr><td><code># report:</code></td><td>One line per satellite; keys below.</td></tr><tr><td><code># impact:</code></td><td>One line per satellite that hit the surface or re-entered; keys below.</td></tr></table></div>\n<h4 id=\"config-reference--columns\">Columns</h4>\n<div class=\"doc-tablewrap\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><tr><th>Column</th><th>Unit</th><th>Meaning</th></tr><tr><td><code>sat_name</code></td><td></td><td>Satellite name.</td></tr><tr><td><code>epoch_utc</code></td><td>ISO 8601, UTC</td><td>Time of the row.</td></tr><tr><td><code>t_s</code></td><td>s</td><td>Seconds since the start epoch.</td></tr><tr><td><code>x_m</code>, <code>y_m</code>, <code>z_m</code></td><td>m</td><td>Position in the inertial frame.</td></tr><tr><td><code>vx_m_s</code>, <code>vy_m_s</code>, <code>vz_m_s</code></td><td>m/s</td><td>Velocity in the inertial frame.</td></tr><tr><td><code>parent_rot_deg</code></td><td>deg</td><td>Prime meridian angle W of the parent: how far it has turned about its pole.</td></tr><tr><td><code>in_shadow</code></td><td></td><td>1 in Earth's cylindrical shadow, 0 sunlit, blank when no Sun is modelled (sphere parents).</td></tr></table></div>\n<p>A satellite that hits the surface or re-enters has no rows after that time.</p>\n<h4 id=\"config-reference--report-keys\">Report keys</h4>\n<div class=\"doc-tablewrap\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><tr><th>Key</th><th>Meaning</th></tr><tr><td><code>energy_rel_drift</code></td><td>Largest relative change in orbital energy over the run, after removing the work of drag, SRP and Sun and Moon gravity and the energy of burns: numerical error alone.</td></tr><tr><td><code>jacobi_rel_drift</code></td><td>The same for the Jacobi integral (sphere parents).</td></tr><tr><td><code>period_s</code>, <code>perigee_alt_m</code>, <code>apogee_alt_m</code></td><td>Of the osculating orbit at the start epoch; altitudes above the equatorial radius.</td></tr><tr><td><code>drag_work_j_per_kg</code></td><td>Work done by drag, when drag is the only extra force.</td></tr><tr><td><code>nongrav_work_j_per_kg</code></td><td>Work done by drag and SRP, when SRP is on (without Sun and Moon gravity).</td></tr><tr><td><code>perturbation_work_j_per_kg</code></td><td>Work done by all extra forces, when Sun and Moon gravity is on.</td></tr><tr><td><code>burns</code>, <code>dv_total_m_s</code></td><td>Number of burns and their total delta-v.</td></tr><tr><td><code>ltan_start</code>, <code>ltan_end</code>, <code>ltan_drift_min_per_day</code></td><td>LTAN at the start and end, and its drift rate (Earth).</td></tr></table></div>\n<p>Impact lines have <code>sat</code>, <code>kind</code> (<code>reentry</code> with drag, <code>impact</code> without), <code>epoch_utc</code>, <code>t_s</code>, <code>body_fixed_m</code> (x y z), <code>lat_deg</code> and <code>lon_deg</code>.</p>\n</section>\n<hr>\n<section>\n<h2 id=\"accuracy\">Accuracy and limits</h2>\n<p>How far the results can be trusted, and how to choose an integration step. The numbers come from the test suite (<code>npm test</code>) and from two comparisons with Orekit, an independent flight-dynamics library: <a href=\"https://github.com/khandelwalkshitij/project-wednesday/blob/main/docs/benchmark-orekit.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">each model in isolation</a>, and <a href=\"https://github.com/khandelwalkshitij/project-wednesday/blob/main/README.md#validation-against-orekit\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">every example config in the app</a>.</p>\n<p>Two kinds of error matter. <b>Numerical error</b> is how far the integrator is from the exact solution of the simulator's own equations; the step setting controls it, and the table below shows how much. <b>Model error</b> is how far those equations are from the real world: an exponential atmosphere instead of the real one, no tesseral gravity and so on. For anything below 1,000 km, model error is usually far larger, especially drag.</p>\n<h3 id=\"accuracy--what-was-checked\">What was checked</h3>\n<div class=\"doc-tablewrap\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><tr><th>Part</th><th>Checked against</th><th>Agreement</th></tr><tr><td>J2-J4 gravity and integrators</td><td>Orekit, 1 to 3 days in LEO, SSO and Molniya orbits</td><td>0.6 to 2.6 mm with DOP853 at rtol 1e-12</td></tr><tr><td>DOP853 integrator</td><td>SciPy's DOP853 (same step count), an analytic Kepler orbit</td><td>0.2 mm after 10 orbits</td></tr><tr><td>SGP4/SDP4 for TLEs</td><td>Vallado's verification vectors; Orekit</td><td>0.1 mm (vectors); 0.03 mm in TEME (Orekit)</td></tr><tr><td>TLE to inertial state</td><td>SGP4 with ERFA's TEME to GCRF</td><td>under 0.1 m. Orekit's TEME definition differs by up to 10 m in GCRF.</td></tr><tr><td>NRLMSISE-00 density</td><td>NRL's original Fortran; Orekit with the same space weather</td><td>8e-5 relative; 1.3e-5 relative</td></tr><tr><td>Drag runs (NRLMSISE-00, 400 km, 1 day)</td><td>Orekit</td><td>5 mm on a quiet day, 0.2 m on the May 2024 storm day</td></tr><tr><td>Sun and Moon gravity</td><td>Orekit (30 days in GEO); the textbook GEO tilt rate</td><td>1.2 cm; 0.95° a year in 2026</td></tr><tr><td>Moon position</td><td>ERFA <code>eraMoon98</code></td><td>under 1 mm (the model itself is good to about 6 km)</td></tr><tr><td>Earth orientation</td><td>ERFA's full IAU 2006/2000A (<code>c2t06a</code>)</td><td>7.5 mm at the surface</td></tr><tr><td>Time scales (UTC, TAI, TT, TDB, GPS)</td><td>ERFA</td><td>0.12 µs</td></tr><tr><td>Energy bookkeeping</td><td>every run reports it</td><td>relative energy drift typically 1e-9 or less</td></tr></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"accuracy--choosing-a-step\">Choosing a step</h3>\n<p>Position error after one day, against DOP853 at rtol 1e-13 (which matches Orekit to millimetres). Produced by <code>node tools/step-table.mjs</code>.</p>\n<div class=\"doc-tablewrap\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><tr><th>Orbit, 1 day</th><th>RK4 1 s</th><th>RK4 5 s</th><th>RK4 10 s</th><th>RK4 30 s</th><th>RK4 60 s</th><th>RK4 120 s</th><th>DOP853 1e-8</th><th>DOP853 1e-10</th><th>DOP853 1e-12</th></tr><tr><td>LEO 400 km</td><td>&lt; 1 mm</td><td>2.4 cm</td><td>51 cm</td><td>82 m</td><td>2.3 km</td><td>68 km</td><td>1.9 m (250 steps)</td><td>0.44 cm (447 steps)</td><td>&lt; 1 mm (798 steps)</td></tr><tr><td>LEO 400 km, drag + SRP</td><td>1.1 cm</td><td>4.1 cm</td><td>59 cm</td><td>86 m</td><td>2.4 km</td><td>69 km</td><td>1.7 m (269 steps)</td><td>1.9 cm (487 steps)</td><td>0.15 cm (836 steps)</td></tr><tr><td>SSO 800 km</td><td>&lt; 1 mm</td><td>1.5 cm</td><td>32 cm</td><td>49 m</td><td>1.4 km</td><td>40 km</td><td>1.7 m (230 steps)</td><td>0.44 cm (411 steps)</td><td>&lt; 1 mm (734 steps)</td></tr><tr><td>Molniya (e = 0.72)</td><td>&lt; 1 mm</td><td>3.2 cm</td><td>53 cm</td><td>50 m</td><td>950 m</td><td>20 km</td><td>21 m (58 steps)</td><td>49 cm (92 steps)</td><td>0.32 cm (154 steps)</td></tr><tr><td>GEO</td><td>&lt; 1 mm</td><td>&lt; 1 mm</td><td>&lt; 1 mm</td><td>&lt; 1 mm</td><td>0.24 cm</td><td>3.8 cm</td><td>84 cm (21 steps)</td><td>0.83 cm (34 steps)</td><td>&lt; 1 mm (56 steps)</td></tr></table></div>\n<p>RK4 at 1 day takes 86,400 / step steps, so 8,640 at 10 s. Errors grow roughly in proportion to run length, mostly along the track.</p>\n<ul><li><b>For most runs, use DOP853 at rtol 1e-10, atol 1e-6.</b> Centimetres a day or better in LEO, in a few hundred steps.</li><li><b>For reference runs or comparisons, use rtol 1e-12.</b></li><li><b>With RK4,</b> 10 s is fine for LEO pictures and charts (tens of centimetres a day). Use 1 to 5 s for precise work. For eccentric orbits the step must suit perigee, not the average: the Molniya row shows this. GEO is forgiving even at 60 s.</li><li><b>With drag and SRP, RK4 cannot do better than about 1 cm a day</b> in LEO however small the step, because the shadow boundary and space-weather changes fall inside its steps. DOP853 lands on them and keeps converging.</li></ul>\n<h3 id=\"accuracy--limits\">Limits</h3>\n<ul><li><b>Gravity:</b> zonal terms J2 to J4 only. Tesseral harmonics are left out; in LEO they cause metres to tens of metres of difference over a day.</li><li><b>Atmosphere:</b> the exponential table has no solar activity, so drag can be off by a factor of several. NRLMSISE-00 follows solar activity, but empirical models typically have 10 to 20% density errors, more in storms. The bundled space-weather table is measured to 20 July 2025; after that it uses CelesTrak's predictions (Ap = 15 where none is predicted), and the run warns.</li><li><b>Sun and Moon positions:</b> analytic, not JPL DE440. The Moon is good to about 6 km and the Sun to about 30 arcseconds (10,000 km). That is plenty for their gravity and for SRP, but not where precise Sun or Moon positions themselves matter.</li><li><b>Shadow:</b> a cylinder with no penumbra, so eclipse entry and exit times are off by up to several seconds.</li><li><b>Earth orientation:</b> no polar motion (about 10 m at the surface), and UT1 is UTC plus a fixed <code>dut1_s</code>. UT1 - UTC is always within 0.9 s, which is up to about 400 m of ground position at the equator if left at 0. Inertial positions, and the orbit itself, are hardly affected; ground tracks and longitudes are.</li><li><b>TLEs:</b> SGP4 sets the starting state; the simulator's own forces carry it from there, so the trajectory drifts from what SGP4 would predict. Measured after one day: 0.25 to 12 km for LEO TLEs, about 170 km for a Molniya TLE (SDP4 includes lunisolar and resonance terms that a short run here does not reproduce from a single state). TLEs themselves are typically good to about a kilometre at their epoch.</li><li><b>Re-entry:</b> a satellite is stopped at 80 km. Below that neither the models nor a point mass are meaningful.</li><li><b>Burns</b> are instantaneous. Long burns lose a little efficiency in reality that is not modelled.</li><li><b>Not modelled:</b> attitude, solar panels or any shape beyond a single area, gravity between satellites, mesh-shaped bodies, relativity, Earth tides.</li><li><b>Size:</b> up to 100 satellites and 1,500,000 output rows per run.</li></ul>\n</section>\n<hr>\n<section>\n<h2 id=\"glossary\">Glossary</h2>\n<p>Plain-language explanations of the terms used in the app and these pages. Most come from the requirement sheets in <code>docs/</code>.</p>\n<div class=\"doc-tablewrap\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr><tr><td>Along-track</td><td>The direction of travel along the orbit.</td></tr><tr><td>Ap</td><td>Geomagnetic activity index for a day; ap is the same for a 3-hour slot. Higher means a more disturbed, hotter upper atmosphere and more drag.</td></tr><tr><td>Apogee, perigee</td><td>The highest and lowest points of an orbit. Altitudes here are above the equatorial radius.</td></tr><tr><td>Argument of periapsis</td><td>Where in its orbit the satellite is lowest, measured from the ascending node.</td></tr><tr><td>atol, rtol</td><td>The error the adaptive integrator allows per step: absolute (metres or m/s) and relative (a fraction of the value).</td></tr><tr><td>Beta angle</td><td>The angle between the Sun and the orbit plane. It sets eclipse length and thermal and power loads.</td></tr><tr><td>Body-fixed</td><td>Coordinates that rotate with the parent body, like latitude and longitude on Earth.</td></tr><tr><td>Burn, delta-v (Δv)</td><td>A change in velocity from a thruster. Impulsive means treated as instant, a good approximation for short burns.</td></tr><tr><td>Cd</td><td>Drag coefficient, about 2.2 for satellites.</td></tr><tr><td>CelesTrak</td><td>A public source of orbital and space-weather data. The bundled space-weather table comes from its SW-All file.</td></tr><tr><td>Cr</td><td>Reflectivity coefficient for solar radiation pressure: 1 absorbs all light, 2 is a mirror.</td></tr><tr><td>Cross-track</td><td>Sideways, out of the orbit plane.</td></tr><tr><td>Cylindrical shadow</td><td>Earth's shadow treated as a straight tube, fully on or off. The real shadow is a cone with a partial-shadow edge (penumbra) lasting a few seconds at entry and exit.</td></tr><tr><td>Dense output</td><td>A smooth curve an integrator provides between its steps, so output rows can fall anywhere without extra steps.</td></tr><tr><td>Differential drag</td><td>Two satellites feeling different drag, so they slowly drift apart. Also usable on purpose to steer formations without fuel.</td></tr><tr><td>DOP853</td><td>A high-accuracy integrator (Dormand-Prince 8th order) that picks its own step size.</td></tr><tr><td>Drag</td><td>Air resistance from the thin upper atmosphere.</td></tr><tr><td>Eccentricity (e)</td><td>How stretched an orbit is: 0 is a circle, close to 1 is a long thin ellipse.</td></tr><tr><td>Eclipse</td><td>Time spent in Earth's shadow. It sets battery depth of discharge.</td></tr><tr><td>Energy drift</td><td>With gravity only, orbital energy should stay exactly constant, so any drift is numerical error: the best overall health check. When drag, SRP or burns change the energy, their work is subtracted first.</td></tr><tr><td>Ephemeris</td><td>A table or formula giving where solar-system bodies are over time. DE440 is JPL's current standard; this simulator uses analytic formulas instead.</td></tr><tr><td>Epoch</td><td>A specific instant in time.</td></tr><tr><td>ERFA</td><td>The open-source standard library for Earth orientation and time scales, derived from the IAU's SOFA.</td></tr><tr><td>F10.7</td><td>Solar radio flux at 10.7 cm, a proxy for how much the Sun heats the upper atmosphere. Drag swings by a factor of 10 over the solar cycle with it.</td></tr><tr><td>Force model</td><td>The list of forces acting on the satellite.</td></tr><tr><td>Geodetic</td><td>Latitude, longitude and altitude measured on the WGS84 ellipsoid, the same as GPS coordinates.</td></tr><tr><td>GCRF, ICRF</td><td>ICRF is the international standard non-rotating frame, fixed relative to distant quasars. GCRF is the same axes centred on Earth. Same idea as J2000, but more precisely defined.</td></tr><tr><td>Ground track</td><td>The path on the surface directly beneath the satellite.</td></tr><tr><td>Inclination (i)</td><td>The tilt of the orbit from the equator.</td></tr><tr><td>Inertial frame</td><td>A non-rotating reference frame, fixed relative to the stars.</td></tr><tr><td>ISO 8601 duration</td><td>A standard way of writing a length of time: P1DT6H means 1 day 6 hours.</td></tr><tr><td>J2, J3, J4</td><td>Correction terms to gravity for Earth's equatorial bulge and north-south lopsidedness. J2 is the big one: it turns orbit planes and moves perigees.</td></tr><tr><td>Jacobi constant</td><td>The conserved quantity that takes the place of energy around a rotating body.</td></tr><tr><td>Leap second</td><td>A second occasionally added to UTC. An outdated table shifts every UTC epoch by 1 s, about 7.5 km of orbit in LEO.</td></tr><tr><td>LTAN</td><td>Local time of the ascending node: the mean local solar time when the satellite crosses the equator going north.</td></tr><tr><td>LVLH</td><td>Local vertical, local horizontal: a frame riding with the satellite. Radial is straight up away from the parent, along-track is the direction of travel, cross-track is sideways out of the orbit plane.</td></tr><tr><td>Mercator</td><td>The standard flat world map projection.</td></tr><tr><td>mu (μ)</td><td>G times the mass of the parent body. μ/r² is gravity at distance r from its centre.</td></tr><tr><td>NRLMSISE-00</td><td>The US Naval Research Laboratory's empirical model of the atmosphere, which follows solar and geomagnetic activity.</td></tr><tr><td>Osculating elements</td><td>The exact instantaneous orbital elements, as opposed to averaged ones like in a TLE.</td></tr><tr><td>Parent</td><td>The body the satellites orbit: Earth or a uniform sphere such as an asteroid.</td></tr><tr><td>Parent rotation angle</td><td>How far the parent has spun, so the surface can be shown turning under the orbit.</td></tr><tr><td>Pole RA/Dec, prime meridian</td><td>The direction of a body's spin axis, given like a star's sky coordinates, and how far the body has rotated at the start: where its longitude zero points.</td></tr><tr><td>Precession, nutation</td><td>The slow wobble of Earth's spin axis. Precession alone moves it about 0.36° between 2000 and 2026, which shifts ground tracks by tens of km if ignored.</td></tr><tr><td>Propagate</td><td>Compute a satellite's position and velocity forward in time.</td></tr><tr><td>RAAN</td><td>Right ascension of the ascending node: where the orbit crosses the equator going north.</td></tr><tr><td>Re-entry</td><td>With drag on, a satellite that falls to 80 km is stopped and reported.</td></tr><tr><td>RK4</td><td>Runge-Kutta 4th order: the standard simple method for stepping equations of motion forward in time with a fixed step.</td></tr><tr><td>Semi-major axis (a)</td><td>Half the long axis of the orbit ellipse: its size.</td></tr><tr><td>SGP4, SDP4</td><td>The standard models for propagating TLEs. SDP4 is the deep-space version for periods of 225 minutes or more.</td></tr><tr><td>Space weather</td><td>Solar flux (F10.7) and geomagnetic activity (Ap) that drive the upper atmosphere.</td></tr><tr><td>SRP</td><td>Solar radiation pressure: the tiny push from sunlight.</td></tr><tr><td>SSO</td><td>Sun-synchronous orbit. Its plane turns 0.9856° a day to keep pace with the Sun, so it passes overhead at the same local time every day.</td></tr><tr><td>State</td><td>A satellite's position and velocity at one instant.</td></tr><tr><td>Subsolar point</td><td>The spot on Earth with the Sun directly overhead.</td></tr><tr><td>TEME</td><td>The frame SGP4 works in (true equator, mean equinox). It is converted to GCRF before the simulation starts.</td></tr><tr><td>Third body</td><td>The gravity of the Sun and Moon.</td></tr><tr><td>Time scales</td><td>UTC is civil time with leap seconds. TAI, TT, TDB and GPS are atomic or astronomical time scales that differ from UTC by tens of seconds, so the scale must always be stated.</td></tr><tr><td>TLE</td><td>Two-line element set: the standard published orbit format.</td></tr><tr><td>Train</td><td>Satellites following each other in the same orbit, evenly spaced.</td></tr><tr><td>True anomaly</td><td>Where the satellite is on its orbit, measured from periapsis.</td></tr><tr><td>Walker pattern</td><td>The standard constellation layout, written i: T/P/F. T total satellites in P orbital planes, with phasing F between neighbouring planes. Example: 53°: 24/3/1.</td></tr><tr><td>WGS84</td><td>The standard Earth shape used by GPS.</td></tr></table></div>\n</section>";
